-- title:
--   Schönhage–Strassen FM procedure: one recursion level is correct within its cost
-- statement:
--   Let `pF`, `pD`, `pT`, `pP` be procedure indices, where `pD`, `pT`, `pP` have bodies `difBody pD`, `ditBody pT`, `pow2Body pP` (forward FFT, inverse FFT, powers of two). Let $N\ge1$ and write $\ell$ = `fmL N` (bit length of $N$), $\beta$ = `fmB N` (bit length of $\ell$), $k=\lfloor \ell/2\rfloor-\beta$, $K=2^k$, $M=\lfloor N/K\rfloor$, $Q=\lfloor (2M+2k+3)/K\rfloor+1$ and $N'=QK$. Assume
--   - (recursion hypothesis) `FMSpec pF iprog I N' C`: calling `pF` multiplies modulo $2^{N'}+1$ any two inputs in $[0,2^{N'}]$ within cost $C$;
--   - the parameter conditions $\beta\le\ell/2$, $KM=N$, $M\ge1$, $2M+2k+4\le N'\le N$ and $\ell\le N$;
--   - `ARG` has $a$ then $b$ on top (over $L_A$) with $0\le a,b\le 2^N$; `PRM` has $N$, `RN` has $2^N$, `RF` has $2^N+1$ on top.
--
--   Then
--   $$\mathrm{IRuns}\ I\ \mathrm{iprog}\ (\mathrm{fmMain}\ pF\ pD\ pT\ pP)\ s\ \big(s[\mathrm{ARG} := (ab \bmod (2^N+1)) :: L_A]\big)\ \big(2^k C + \mathrm{fmLevelCost}(N)\big).$$
--   All other stacks are restored exactly.
--
--   `fmMain` folds the inputs, computes the parameters, splits each input into $K$ pieces of $M$ bits, twists them by powers of $2^Q$, applies the forward FFT over $\mathbb Z/(2^{N'}+1)$, multiplies pointwise by $K$ recursive calls, applies the inverse FFT, untwists, lifts to signed coefficients, propagates carries and recombines. The bound $\mathrm{fmLevelCost}(N) = 2\,\mathrm{difCost}+\mathrm{ditCost}+O(K N'+\ell^2+N)$ is $O(N\log N)$; solving the resulting recurrence gives the potential bound $A\,N\ell\lambda$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** States are `IState NS` records with item stacks `G : Fin NS → List Item` (head = top), input pointer `ip` and output `out`; `s.upd X L` replaces stack `X` by `L`; `ints A` is the list of integer items of `A`. The named stacks (`ARG`, `PRM`, `RN`, `RF`, `DX`, `DL`, …) are fixed indices in `Fin 32`. `IRuns I iprog p s s' c` means that `p` executes from `s` to exactly `s'` with some cost $c' \le c$, so costs are upper bounds; the program text `iprog` and input `I` are arbitrary unless constrained.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.fmMain_runs` of the Lean development NLogN/SSFM5.lean (lines 38–244), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

